      "raw": "<p>It would be great to get an official ruling (and a general flowchart of how certain play actions get resolved). Even after seeing this post, we&#39;re still having a debate in our group whether the interpretation is correct.</p><p>The debate generally centers on a couple of different things.</p><p>First, the FAQ has a section that says that a card is still in effect during is destruction, meaning that the card still triggers its power because it is still alive to be triggered by damage.</p><p>The second argument, along the same lines, is that damaging something is a distinct action from destroying it (because among other things, damage can occur and even reduce something to 0 without destroying it, if the right cards are in play.) I think the closest we&#39;ve got to a definitive ruling here would be the game play for baron blade in the computer game; I believe if you take him out while he has backlash field up, it goes right to end game.</p><p>Anyway, the difference in opinion over these greatly affects the power level of Captain Cosmic.</p><p>Here&#39;s a stab at writing these interpretations as a series of steps</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>Interpretation 1</p><p>1. Apply damage</p><p>2. Check if target died</p><p>3. If target died, apply any cards or effects based on the cards destruction, and then remove it from play</p><p>4. Apply effects based on the card taking damage.</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>interpretation 2</p><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">1. Apply damage</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">2.&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">Apply effects based on the card taking damage.</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">3. Check if target died</span></p><p><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">4.&nbsp;</span><span style=\"font-size: 13px; line-height: 20px;\">If target died, apply any cards or effects based on the cards destruction, and then remove it from play</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
